== fuller page ==
What if an application on the wheel wants floors of its own? Corwin has
answered that "several times": "The higher run-levels are virtual allowing
applications running on the ghostwheel to simulate tumbler change as for lower
run-levels while staying in the "running" model from a host system
standpoint" [virtual].

So the roof does not move. The host still reads running. Above it, an
application can climb pretend floors of its own and turn the same kinds of
locks for its own purposes [above].

One tool already rides them. ix0xi, the second index, calls itself an
elevator. It quotes him: "virtual run levels above our max of four (>4 is all
just RUNNING, to us ...)", so that moving among them "can simulate alternate
index by ADDING LEADING DOTS" to a path [elevator]. One floor up, c4.m.map
becomes .c4.m.map; two floors up, ..c4.m.map. That one dot means one floor is
the tool author's reading, and the tool says so [dot].

Pin has two notes here that differ by one floor. The ruling's file puts
running at 5 and the pretend floors above it [five]. The elevator counts 1, 2,
3 and MANY as real, and 5, 6, 7 as virtual [many]. From the host's side they
agree: past 4, it is all running.

The ruling's file says why it was written down at all: "A ruling that has to
be re-answered is a memory failure, not a question" [memory].

